/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   A token resolving to 69 in a leading comment opens a nested comment and
   swallows the rest of the file - see RMSTEST_56 and 57. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>

  /* Branch on PLAYER THREE, not player one. That is the entire point of this
     file - see the header. */
  if PLAYER3_TEAM1
    base_terrain DESERT
  elseif PLAYER3_TEAM2
    base_terrain SNOW
  else
    base_terrain GRASS
  endif

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_59 - confirm canonical team numbering on a player who is NOT player one
Feeds: preview-design Sec.3.1 step 3, src/generationSettings/teamModel.ts,
       Sec.15 item 17(a)

WHY THIS EXISTS WHEN 17(a) IS ALREADY CLOSED
RMSTEST_44 refuted the rival model across three discriminating lobbies, and that
is what decided the code. But it cannot confirm the surviving one, and the reason
is structural rather than a gap in how it was run.

Under lowest-player-number, player one is BY DEFINITION the lowest-numbered
player, so their team is always canonical team 1 and PLAYER1_TEAM1 is a
tautology. A script branching on that label returns the same answer under the
real rule and under "this label is simply always defined". 44 cannot tell those
apart and neither can any number of extra lobbies run through it.

Player three carries no such guarantee. The two models put them on DIFFERENT
canonical teams, and neither prediction is trivially true.

THE LOBBY
Four players, lobby order as listed:

  player 1 -> team 2
  player 2 -> team 2
  player 3 -> team 1
  player 4 -> team 1

This is RMSTEST_44's first discriminating configuration, unchanged, so the two
runs are directly comparable and the pair reads as one experiment.

  lowest player number      - player 1's team is canonical 1, so player 3's team
                              is canonical 2 -> PLAYER3_TEAM2 -> SNOW
  ascending selected number - selected team 1 holds players 3 and 4 and sorts
                              first, so it is canonical 1 -> PLAYER3_TEAM1 ->
                              DESERT

WHAT YOU WILL SEE

  WHITE map (snow)    -> PLAYER3_TEAM2. Lowest-player-number CONFIRMED on a
                         non-tautological label. Sec.3.1 step 3 is measured
                         rather than merely unrefuted, and 17(a) needs nothing
                         further.
  YELLOW map (desert) -> PLAYER3_TEAM1. That contradicts RMSTEST_44's three runs
                         and means the numbering is not one rule applied to all
                         players. Do not patch teamModel.ts against it - the
                         derivation would need rebuilding from both results.
  GREEN map (grass)   -> neither label defined. Then the player-in-team family
                         does not fire the way both models assume, which is the
                         outcome 44's own header named and never saw.

Note the colours mean the OPPOSITE of what they mean in 44, because the label
that indicates the surviving model is TEAM2 here and TEAM1 there. That is
deliberate and it is stated in both files: a matched colour scheme would make
the confirming run look like a repeat of the refuting one.

GENERATE AT TINY (120) FROM A REAL GAME LOBBY. ONE GAME.

Nothing to save and nothing to probe. The editor cannot set team numbers, and a
lobby save is game state rather than a scenario, so the reading is the minimap.
Screenshot it if a record is wanted.
*/
